Introduction
Most of the momentum tools available natively in MQL5 come from finite differencing: subtract yesterday's price from today's, divide by the time step, call it velocity. Do it twice and call it acceleration. It works, but differencing amplifies noise badly — every tick of market microstructure noise gets multiplied, not averaged out, and a second difference multiplies it again. The Savitzky-Golay filter exists precisely to fix this: fit a low-degree polynomial to a local window of data by least squares, then read the value and derivatives off the fitted polynomial itself instead of off the raw, noisy samples.
The textbook Savitzky-Golay filter is centered: its fitting window uses samples from both before and after the estimated point. This is fine offline, but impossible on a live chart because the "future" bars do not exist yet. This article builds the one-sided variant instead. The fitting window is entirely trailing, made up of bars that have already closed, and the polynomial is evaluated only at the window's own rightmost point: the most recently closed bar. Nothing computed for a closed bar can ever change once that bar closes, because nothing after it was ever part of the computation. That is the non-repainting property this article sets out to prove, not just assert.
Scope note
. The main contribution of this article is CSavGolFilter, a reusable causal local-polynomial estimator, together with an indicator and a self-test script. The accompanying EA is a deliberately simple demonstration of how the filter can be used in a trading rule, not a validated or optimized strategy. Its test results and practical limitations are discussed in the corresponding sections below.
Every quantitative claim is backed by a specific check. Kernel identities are verified on a synthetic polynomial. Non-repainting extraction is checked on real history using two parameterizations of the same API. The demo EA is backtested on three instrument/timeframe combinations, and all results are reported as obtained, including failures.
The primitive built here is not limited to the entry/exit state machine this article demonstrates it with. A non-repainting velocity is a reasonable input to a breakout confirmation filter (did price actually accelerate through the level, or just touch it); a non-repainting acceleration is a candidate input to a volatility-aware trailing-stop distance, since a decelerating move is a different risk profile from an accelerating one even at the same instantaneous velocity. Neither of those is built or tested in this article — they are mentioned only to be clear that CSavGolFilter itself, not the specific EA built on top of it, is the reusable contribution here.
Architecture
The system has one piece of real math and three consumers of it. The math lives entirely in SavGolCore.mqh's CSavGolFilter class, which solves for a fixed set of convolution weights once per (window length, polynomial order, time step) combination and then answers every subsequent bar with a plain dot product. The three consumers are an indicator that plots velocity and acceleration, an EA that trades on their sign agreement, and a self-test script that verifies the class does what its own header box claims.
Pipeline from a closed-bar window to its three consumers
Fig. 1. Architecture: one shared CSavGolFilter instance feeding an indicator, an EA, and a self-test script.
The reason the design separates "solve for the kernels" ( Init ) from "apply the kernels" ( Apply ) is cost. Solving the normal equations involves a matrix transpose, a matrix product, and a matrix inverse — not something you want to repeat on every incoming tick. Because the kernels depend only on the window length, the polynomial order, and the bar period, and never on the price data itself, they can be solved exactly once and reused for the life of the chart. Apply() afterwards is three running sums over a window of at most a few dozen numbers, cheap enough to call on every closed bar without a second thought.
SavGolCore.mqh: Solving for the Kernels
