Two design decisions determine whether this fit is correct and well-behaved. The first is what x_i means: setting x_i = i, with i=0 at the oldest bar, is the more obvious indexing and the wrong one — a polynomial's coefficient c0 is p(0), the fitted value at wherever x=0 sits, so an oldest-bar-at-zero basis gives the value and slope AT THE OLDEST BAR, not the bar the filter is meant to describe. Centering the basis on the evaluation point instead avoids this, so x=0 lands on the newest bar and every older bar gets a negative offset.
The second decision is scale, and it is purely about numerical conditioning, not correctness. Centering alone still leaves x_i ranging from -(N-1) to 0, so the design matrix's column magnitudes grow with window length, and so does V^T V's own scale. Dividing x through by N-1 before raising it to any power keeps x bounded to [-1, 0] regardless of window size; the chain rule then needs one extra factor of that scale per derivative order to get back to real units.
