Two things are worth being precise about here. The guard above checks window<=order+1, not merely window<=order, since window==order+1 makes the design matrix square — exact interpolation, not an overdetermined fit, and is rejected for the same reason an underdetermined one is. The determinant threshold below it is still not a genuine condition-number test; a small-but-nonzero determinant can coexist with a badly conditioned matrix in principle. What normalizing x buys is narrower but real and measured: run the same math outside MQL5 at order 2, unnormalized det(V^T V) grows from ~3.6×10^8 at a 21-bar window to ~5.1×10^14 at 101 bars, with condition number growing from ~2.7×10^5 to ~1.8×10^8 — a fixed 1e-12 threshold would reject almost nothing at the larger size regardless of true conditioning. Normalized, both stay roughly flat across the same range (determinant 5.7-506, condition number ~440-500), which is what makes one fixed threshold mean the same thing at any window length. More in Limitations below.
Normalizing changes none of the kernel VALUES that come out the other end — verified, not just argued, by running both bases through the same window/order and confirming the kernels match to floating-point precision; only the intermediate solve's conditioning differs. The centering choice itself protects something specific: a quadratic's acceleration term is a constant ( 2*c2, independent of evaluation point), so an x=i version centered at the oldest bar would still get acceleration right by coincidence while silently getting value and velocity wrong. That split is the exact failure signature SGKinematics_SelfTest.mq5 's identity check is built to catch, discussed in its own section below.
CSavGolFilter::Apply: What the Kernels Actually Look Like
Once Init() has solved for the three kernels, applying them is deliberately uninteresting: three running sums over the same window, guarded by a size check so a caller with an off-by-one in its own rolling-buffer bookkeeping gets a clean false back instead of silently reading garbage.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CSavGolFilter::Apply                                             |
//| Applies the cached kernels to one trailing window of prices. This|
//| is the function called once per closed bar by the indicator, the |
//| EA, and the self-test script -- it never allocates, never touches|
//| market data itself, and has no notion of time, which is exactly  |
//| what keeps it reusable across all three.                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
