It is worth actually looking at what these kernels contain, rather than taking "convolution kernel" on faith. For the project's default window of 21 bars and polynomial order 2, the three kernels look like this:
The three solved kernels for window=21, order=2
Fig. 2. Kernel shapes: the value kernel is a smooth, slightly front-loaded weighting; the velocity kernel is antisymmetric with the heaviest weight on the newest bars; the acceleration kernel is U-shaped, weighting both ends of the window against the middle.
The value kernel sums to exactly 1.0 — a constant input signal must be reproduced unchanged by any least-squares polynomial fit, and that identity is one of the two checks SGKinematics_SelfTest.mq5 runs before anything else. The velocity kernel is close to antisymmetric around the window's center of mass but skews its heaviest weights toward the newest bars, which makes sense: those are the bars closest to, and most informative about, the point the derivative is actually being evaluated at. None of this shape is data-dependent — it falls straight out of the window length and polynomial order chosen at Init() time, which is exactly why solving it once and reusing it is correct.
"Better-behaved than differencing" deserves an actual number, not just a design argument. Standard noise-gain analysis: any derivative estimate that is a fixed linear combination of window prices, sum(w_i * p_i), has noise variance σ² * sum(w_i²) against independent per-bar noise of variance σ². The naive two-point difference (weights [1,-1]/dt ) and three-point second difference (weights [1,-2,1]/dt² ) give noise variances 2σ²/dt² and 6σ²/dt⁴ respectively. Summing the squared kernel weights from Fig. 2 for window=21, order=2 and comparing directly: the SG velocity kernel's noise variance is roughly 99.0% lower than the naive difference's, and the acceleration kernel's roughly 99.997% lower — the real, computed reason local polynomial fitting outperforms direct differencing on noisy input.
This is an analytic noise-gain comparison, not a backtested one, so it says nothing about the smoothing lag a 21-bar window also introduces. The table below is the actual trace behind those two percentages, computed directly from the same kernel-solving code shown in the SavGolCore.mqh section above (dt=1 throughout, since it cancels out of every ratio):
