The same evaluation-point-centering argument generalizes past order 2, even though this article only ships value/velocity/acceleration (orders 0, 1 and 2). For a polynomial of any degree
d
fitted with the centered basis, the k-th derivative at the evaluation point is k! · c_k. Extracting a third derivative (jerk) from an order-3 fit would follow the identical pattern — one more cached kernel, P.Row(3) * 6.0 / dt^3 — but it is not something this shipped Apply() computes: its signature is fixed to exactly the three outputs this article uses, and adding a fourth would mean widening that signature, not just adding a kernel.
SGKinematics.mq5: The Indicator
The indicator exposes three buffers but only plots two of them. Velocity and acceleration are on a completely different numeric scale from price, so overlaying the smoothed value on the chart alongside them would make one or the other unreadable. The smoothed value is kept as a hidden calculation buffer instead, reachable from an external script or EA via iCustom()/CopyBuffer() by MQL5's documented buffer-registration mechanism — scope note in Limitations below.
