OnCalculate() is what makes the non-repainting claim auditable rather than asserted, and it rests on two separate things, not one. The first is the loop bound itself. A naive incremental loop runs through every index up to rates_total-1 inclusive — which includes the still-forming current bar, since rates_total counts it. Plotting that bar would not quite be a bug (any live indicator's newest point is expected to move until the bar closes), but it sits awkwardly next to "only closed bars are shown," and a casual reader could call it repainting even though the closed bars behind it never change. closed_total = rates_total-1 removes the ambiguity instead: the forming bar's three buffer slots are explicitly set to EMPTY_VALUE every call, and the main loop is bounded at closed_total, never rates_total.
The second is the indexing direction of OnCalculate()'s own price-array parameters. Every index this function computes — closed_total, the loop's start, idx inside the inner window-fill loop — assumes window_prices[0] lands on the oldest bar and the forming bar sits at rates_total-1. That assumption only holds if time[], open[], high[], low[] and close[] are indexed oldest-to-newest, so the function fixes that explicitly with ArraySetAsSeries(...,false) on each one rather than relying on whatever direction they would otherwise arrive in. With both of those in place, the indicator computes, stores, and draws values only for closed bars, with no exceptions. For every bar i that IS computed, the loop builds that bar's OWN trailing window ending at i, never touching anything beyond it.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| OnCalculate                                                      |
//| Recomputes only the bars a normal incremental indicator would: on|
//| the very first call every closed bar from InpWindow-1 onward; on |
