Getting the inner index backwards here — putting the newest bar at window_prices[0] instead of window_prices[InpWindow-1] — would not crash anything, but it reuses kernels solved for one time direction on data running the other way, and that is not simply a sign flip. The kernels are always evaluated at the same grid position (x=0, index InpWindow-1), so handing them a reversed window is equivalent to fitting p(-1-x) instead of p(x) and reading the result off at that same spot — which lands on what was originally the window's oldest bar, not its newest. Velocity comes out as the negative of the original derivative at the oldest bar, not the negated derivative at the newest one, and for anything above a straight-line fit those two generally have different magnitudes, not just opposite signs.
Acceleration is the one piece that survives this unscathed for the default order=2 fit, for any input, not merely as a coincidence of symmetric test data: a quadratic's second derivative is the constant term 2*c2, the same wherever it's evaluated, so reading it off the mirrored fit returns exactly the same value. That invariance is specific to order 2 — a cubic or higher fit's second derivative varies across the window, so the same reversal would generally change it too.
The non-repainting self-test in SGKinematics_SelfTest.mq5, discussed in its own section below, is designed to surface exactly that class of indexing error — though it is worth being precise that the self-test exercises CSavGolFilter::Apply() and its own window-slicing directly, not this file's OnCalculate() itself; the closed-bars-only guarantee described above is a property of this loop's own bounds, confirmed by reading the code, not something the self-test separately re-verifies against this specific file. OnDeinit() has nothing to release — the buffers and the CSavGolFilter member both clean up after themselves when the indicator unloads.
SGKinematics_EA.mq5: Trading the Kinematics
The EA exists to give the filter one concrete, testable consumer beyond an indicator overlay. Its trading rule is deliberately simple: classify velocity and acceleration by sign on every closed bar, enter when acceleration has just turned in the same direction velocity already points (momentum building on an existing drift, not merely price moving), and exit on either deceleration against the position or an outright velocity reversal.
